Myth: Long ago, the Sky Goddess completed her greatest work—stars that twinkled and danced with wind sprites across the heavens. She admired them each night, content with her creation.

But the Moon, newly formed and observing from afar, felt deeply envious. Unlike the playful stars with their dancing companions, the Moon traveled alone through the sky, her light cold and constant.

One night, she confronted the Sky Goddess. "Why do the stars receive sprites to dance with while I journey in solitude?"

The Sky Goddess considered this carefully. "The stars are many and small—they need the sprites to bring them life. But you, dear Moon, are already alive. Your loneliness itself is your power."

"How can loneliness be power?" the Moon asked, confused.

"Watch," said the Sky Goddess, gesturing below.

The Moon looked down and saw lovers walking beneath her light, poets composing by her glow, and travelers guided by her steady presence. While the stars inspired wonder with their twinkling dance, she provided something deeper—constancy, reliability, a faithful companion to all who felt alone.

Understanding bloomed within the Moon. She was not cursed with solitude; she was blessed to be a mirror for all who journeyed through their own darkness.
